1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:08,520
Have strong suspicions that Crippen London cellar murderer and accomplice are among saloon passengers.

2
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:10,952
Mustache taken off growing beard.

3
00:00:10,952 --> 00:00:12,869
Accomplice dressed as boy.

4
00:00:12,869 --> 00:00:15,523
Manner and build undoubtedly a girl.

5
00:00:15,974 --> 00:00:23,284
Twenty-nine words, tapped out in Morse code from a ship heading west across the Atlantic, in July 1910.

6
00:00:23,284 --> 00:00:28,537
Within days, they would be on the front page of every newspaper in London.

7
00:00:28,537 --> 00:00:33,292
And no fugitive, it is said, had ever been caught like this before.

8
00:00:33,743 --> 00:00:41,729
On one side of the ocean, a quiet American doctor and a young woman dressed as a boy, travelling as father and son.

9
00:00:41,729 --> 00:00:48,189
On the other, a detective from Scotland Yard, on a faster ship, racing to reach Canada first.

10
00:00:48,639 --> 00:00:54,680
Behind them, in a house in north London, the police had found something under a cellar floor.

11
00:00:54,680 --> 00:01:00,917
And the man everyone was looking for had already been questioned once, and allowed to walk away.

12
00:01:01,366 --> 00:01:09,838
This is the story of the Crippen case, and the first time wireless telegraphy helped to catch a man wanted for murder.

13
00:01:09,838 --> 00:01:11,632
This is Precinct Archive.

14
00:01:13,483 --> 00:01:18,131
Hawley Harvey Crippen was born in 1862 in Coldwater, Michigan.

15
00:01:18,131 --> 00:01:25,028
He trained in homeopathy, graduating from the Cleveland Homeopathic Medical College in 1884.

16
00:01:25,028 --> 00:01:34,250
He was a small, quiet, bespectacled man, and the people who knew him would later struggle to describe him as anything else.

17
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:37,932
His first wife, Charlotte, died in 1892.

18
00:01:37,932 --> 00:01:41,731
Two years later, in New York, he married again.

19
00:01:41,731 --> 00:01:50,379
His second wife had been born Kunigunde Mackamotski, called herself Cora Turner, and wanted to be a singer.

20
00:01:50,379 --> 00:01:54,501
On stage, she would use another name: Belle Elmore.

21
00:01:54,950 --> 00:02:02,899
In 1897 the couple moved to London, where Crippen was to manage the British office of a patent medicine company.

22
00:02:02,899 --> 00:02:06,022
There was a problem he could not get around.

23
00:02:06,022 --> 00:02:11,770
His American qualifications did not allow him to practise as a doctor in Britain.

24
00:02:11,770 --> 00:02:18,513
So he sold remedies instead, by post and over the counter, and moved from one firm to the next.

25
00:02:18,962 --> 00:02:21,705
Cora threw herself into the music hall.

26
00:02:21,705 --> 00:02:25,713
Her stage career was modest, but her social life was not.

27
00:02:25,713 --> 00:02:33,589
She became a familiar face among the variety performers of the day, and she joined the Music Hall Ladies' Guild.

28
00:02:33,589 --> 00:02:36,612
Her friends were loyal, loud and observant.

29
00:02:36,612 --> 00:02:41,323
Hold on to that, because it is the reason this story was ever told.

30
00:02:41,774 --> 00:02:49,269
In 1900, at the Drouet Institute for the Deaf, Crippen hired a young typist named Ethel Le Neve.

31
00:02:49,269 --> 00:02:55,280
She was seventeen, from Diss in Norfolk, and the daughter of a railway clerk.

32
00:02:55,280 --> 00:03:00,355
By 1905, according to the sources, the two were having an affair.

33
00:03:00,355 --> 00:03:02,697
By 1908, she was his mistress.

34
00:03:03,147 --> 00:03:09,601
By then the Crippens were living at number 39 Hilldrop Crescent, off Camden Road in Holloway.

35
00:03:09,601 --> 00:03:16,193
It was a large house for a man on a modest income, and they took in lodgers to help pay for it.

36
00:03:16,193 --> 00:03:19,663
By most accounts the marriage was not a happy one.

37
00:03:19,663 --> 00:03:23,341
Each of them, it is reported, had found someone else.

38
00:03:23,790 --> 00:03:30,473
On Monday, the 31st of January, 1910, the Crippens gave a small dinner party at Hilldrop Crescent.

39
00:03:30,473 --> 00:03:34,224
Two guests from the music hall world came to the house.

40
00:03:34,224 --> 00:03:37,224
They left in the early hours of the morning.

41
00:03:37,224 --> 00:03:41,998
They were among the last people known to have seen Cora Crippen alive.

42
00:03:42,448 --> 00:03:46,538
In the days that followed, Cora did not appear at the Guild.

43
00:03:46,538 --> 00:03:52,196
Crippen sent word that she had been called away to America, to see a sick relative.

44
00:03:52,196 --> 00:03:55,332
Then he said she had fallen ill there herself.

45
00:03:55,332 --> 00:04:02,014
Then, in March, he announced that she had died in California, and that her body had been cremated.

46
00:04:02,463 --> 00:04:05,545
Something else was happening at the same time.

47
00:04:05,545 --> 00:04:08,426
Ethel Le Neve moved into Hilldrop Crescent.

48
00:04:08,426 --> 00:04:19,548
And she was seen in public wearing Cora's furs and Cora's jewellery, including at a dinner given by the Guild, where Cora's friends could see exactly what she had on.

49
00:04:19,997 --> 00:04:22,359
Cora's friends did not believe him.

50
00:04:22,359 --> 00:04:29,037
Among them was the strongwoman Kate Williams, who performed as Vulcana, and who went to the police.

51
00:04:29,037 --> 00:04:31,129
The police did little at first.

52
00:04:31,129 --> 00:04:40,438
Then the actress Lil Hawthorne and her husband, John Nash, came back from a tour of America, where they had heard nothing about any death.

53
00:04:40,438 --> 00:04:47,791
Nash knew a senior officer at Scotland Yard, Superintendent Frank Froest, and he pressed him to look into it.

54
00:04:48,242 --> 00:04:51,374
The case went to Chief Inspector Walter Dew.

55
00:04:51,374 --> 00:04:54,079
He was a detective of long experience.

56
00:04:54,079 --> 00:04:59,703
In 1888 he had been a young constable in Whitechapel during the Ripper murders.

57
00:04:59,703 --> 00:05:04,045
By 1910 he had more than a hundred commendations to his name.

58
00:05:04,495 --> 00:05:09,273
On the 8th of July, 1910, Dew went to see Crippen at his office.

59
00:05:09,273 --> 00:05:12,035
And Crippen did something unexpected.

60
00:05:12,035 --> 00:05:14,200
He admitted that he had lied.

61
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:16,215
Cora was not dead, he said.

62
00:05:16,215 --> 00:05:23,008
She had left him, for an American music hall performer named Bruce Miller, and gone abroad.

63
00:05:23,008 --> 00:05:27,561
He had invented her death to spare himself the embarrassment.

64
00:05:28,012 --> 00:05:33,931
Crippen went with Dew to Hilldrop Crescent and let him look around, cellar included.

65
00:05:33,931 --> 00:05:35,199
Dew found nothing.

66
00:05:35,199 --> 00:05:38,582
By his own account he left reasonably satisfied.

67
00:05:38,582 --> 00:05:41,964
A wife who had left her husband was not a crime.

68
00:05:41,964 --> 00:05:45,488
Crippen agreed to help place a notice to find her.

69
00:05:45,937 --> 00:05:50,519
A few days later, Dew went back with some routine questions.

70
00:05:50,519 --> 00:05:51,817
Crippen was gone.

71
00:05:51,817 --> 00:05:53,421
So was Ethel Le Neve.

72
00:05:53,421 --> 00:06:01,209
A man who had just told the police a credible story had run, and in running he had changed everything.

73
00:06:01,209 --> 00:06:03,806
The police went back to the house.

74
00:06:05,656 --> 00:06:09,190
They searched Hilldrop Crescent three more times.

75
00:06:09,190 --> 00:06:16,116
On the fourth search, on the 12th and 13th of July, Dew turned his attention to the coal cellar.

76
00:06:16,116 --> 00:06:19,218
Some of the bricks in the floor were loose.

77
00:06:19,218 --> 00:06:24,412
Beneath them, in a layer of lime and clay, officers found human remains.

78
00:06:24,862 --> 00:06:26,824
What they found was partial.

79
00:06:26,824 --> 00:06:30,888
The head, the limbs and the skeleton were never recovered.

80
00:06:30,888 --> 00:06:39,718
With the remains were hair curlers, strands of bleached hair, and a fragment of a man's pyjama jacket bearing a maker's label.

81
00:06:39,718 --> 00:06:43,853
A missing person inquiry had become a murder investigation.

82
00:06:44,302 --> 00:06:51,656
The Home Office analyst, William Willcox, examined the remains and found hyoscine, a powerful sedative.

83
00:06:51,656 --> 00:06:59,437
Records showed that Crippen had bought hyoscine from a chemist in January, shortly before Cora was last seen.

84
00:06:59,437 --> 00:07:05,149
On the 16th of July, warrants were issued for the arrest of Crippen and Le Neve.

85
00:07:05,598 --> 00:07:08,510
The story went straight onto the front pages.

86
00:07:08,510 --> 00:07:13,943
Their photographs were printed under headlines about the North London cellar murder.

87
00:07:13,943 --> 00:07:22,029
Every reader in Britain was now looking for a small man with a moustache and gold-rimmed glasses, and a young woman with him.

88
00:07:22,480 --> 00:07:25,117
But Crippen was no longer in Britain.

89
00:07:25,117 --> 00:07:30,036
The day after Dew's first visit, he and Ethel had crossed to Belgium.

90
00:07:30,036 --> 00:07:35,240
They spent a night in a Brussels hotel, then went to the port of Antwerp.

91
00:07:35,240 --> 00:07:38,092
On the way, he shaved off his moustache.

92
00:07:38,092 --> 00:07:41,157
Ethel cut her hair and put on a boy's suit.

93
00:07:41,607 --> 00:07:47,817
On the 20th of July, they boarded the Canadian Pacific liner Montrose, bound for Quebec.

94
00:07:47,817 --> 00:07:55,086
They travelled in second-class saloon accommodation, as a father and son: Mr John Robinson and his boy.

95
00:07:55,536 --> 00:07:59,411
The captain of the Montrose was Henry George Kendall.

96
00:07:59,411 --> 00:08:04,016
He was thirty-six, a career seaman, and he read the newspapers.

97
00:08:04,016 --> 00:08:11,472
He had also worked alongside Guglielmo Marconi on ship-to-shore radio before taking his first command.

98
00:08:11,472 --> 00:08:12,935
That detail matters.

99
00:08:13,384 --> 00:08:15,253
Kendall noticed Mr Robinson.

100
00:08:15,253 --> 00:08:26,201
In the captain's own later account, the man had the mark of spectacles on his nose but wore none, and the skin above his lip suggested a moustache recently removed.

101
00:08:26,201 --> 00:08:27,803
He noticed the son, too.

102
00:08:27,803 --> 00:08:37,015
The boy's trousers were held together with safety pins, and the way the young passenger moved struck the captain as unmistakably feminine.

103
00:08:37,015 --> 00:08:44,625
Kendall reportedly also saw the two holding hands, which seemed an odd thing for a father and a teenage son to do.

104
00:08:45,076 --> 00:08:46,587
So he tested the idea.

105
00:08:46,587 --> 00:08:53,726
According to accounts, he took one of the newspaper photographs and covered the moustache with his hand.

106
00:08:53,726 --> 00:09:02,239
And he tried a small trick: he called out to "Mr Robinson", and the man did not respond at once, as if it were not his name.

107
00:09:02,688 --> 00:09:04,426
Here is the turning point.

108
00:09:04,426 --> 00:09:10,172
The Montrose carried a Marconi wireless set, and a telegraphist named Lawrence Hughes.

109
00:09:10,172 --> 00:09:16,653
The set had a limited range, and the ship would soon be beyond reach of the transmitters on land.

110
00:09:16,653 --> 00:09:22,332
Kendall wrote out a message and had Hughes send it to the line's office in Liverpool.

111
00:09:22,783 --> 00:09:30,221
Have strong suspicions that Crippen London cellar murderer and accomplice are among saloon passengers.

112
00:09:30,221 --> 00:09:32,628
Mustache taken off growing beard.

113
00:09:32,628 --> 00:09:34,524
Accomplice dressed as boy.

114
00:09:34,524 --> 00:09:37,149
Manner and build undoubtedly a girl.

115
00:09:37,600 --> 00:09:40,230
The message was passed to Scotland Yard.

116
00:09:40,230 --> 00:09:46,808
It is widely described as the first time that wireless telegraphy was used to help catch a criminal.

117
00:09:46,808 --> 00:09:50,426
And it put Walter Dew in front of a simple calculation.

118
00:09:50,876 --> 00:09:54,222
The Montrose was a steady ship, not a fast one.

119
00:09:54,222 --> 00:09:57,283
She was already days out into the Atlantic.

120
00:09:57,283 --> 00:10:02,409
But the White Star liner Laurentic, sailing from Liverpool, was quicker.

121
00:10:02,409 --> 00:10:06,467
If Dew could get aboard her, he might reach Canada first.

122
00:10:06,467 --> 00:10:08,745
He went to Liverpool and sailed.

123
00:10:09,193 --> 00:10:12,581
What happened next was unusual for another reason.

124
00:10:12,581 --> 00:10:18,680
Kendall kept sending reports as long as he could, and the newspapers learned of the chase.

125
00:10:18,680 --> 00:10:25,049
For days, readers in Britain followed a pursuit that the man being pursued knew nothing about.

126
00:10:25,049 --> 00:10:33,723
On board the Montrose, Crippen, by the accounts that survive, read, walked the deck, and remarked on the marvel of the wireless.

127
00:10:34,173 --> 00:10:38,516
Two ships, one ocean, and a gap closing by the hour.

128
00:10:38,516 --> 00:10:40,938
Could Dew reach Quebec first?

129
00:10:42,788 --> 00:10:43,458
He could.

130
00:10:43,458 --> 00:10:50,822
By his own account, Dew landed in Canada on the 29th of July, two days before the Montrose was due.

131
00:10:50,822 --> 00:10:55,955
He contacted the Canadian authorities, and together they made a plan.

132
00:10:56,404 --> 00:11:03,251
Ships entering the St Lawrence stopped near Father Point, on the south shore, to take on a river pilot.

133
00:11:03,251 --> 00:11:05,511
A pilot coming aboard was routine.

134
00:11:05,511 --> 00:11:07,637
Nobody on deck would look twice.

135
00:11:08,086 --> 00:11:13,846
On Sunday, the 31st of July, 1910, a pilot boat came out to the Montrose.

136
00:11:13,846 --> 00:11:20,395
Among the men who climbed aboard was Walter Dew, wearing a pilot's cap and uniform.

137
00:11:20,395 --> 00:11:23,472
With him was a Canadian police officer.

138
00:11:23,923 --> 00:11:26,147
Captain Kendall had arranged it.

139
00:11:26,147 --> 00:11:29,693
He invited Mr Robinson to come and meet the pilots.

140
00:11:29,693 --> 00:11:32,752
Crippen walked up, and Dew took off his cap.

141
00:11:33,202 --> 00:11:34,881
"Good morning, Dr Crippen.

142
00:11:34,881 --> 00:11:35,849
Do you know me?

143
00:11:35,849 --> 00:11:38,689
I'm Chief Inspector Dew from Scotland Yard."

144
00:11:39,139 --> 00:11:40,430
There was a pause.

145
00:11:40,430 --> 00:11:45,164
Then, according to the record, Crippen said: "Thank God it's over.

146
00:11:45,164 --> 00:11:47,460
The suspense has been too great.

147
00:11:47,460 --> 00:11:49,755
I couldn't stand it any longer."

148
00:11:49,755 --> 00:11:52,697
He held out his wrists for the handcuffs.

149
00:11:53,145 --> 00:11:56,631
Ethel Le Neve was arrested in her cabin soon afterwards.

150
00:11:56,631 --> 00:12:02,794
Because Canada was then a dominion within the British Empire, there was no long extradition battle.

151
00:12:02,794 --> 00:12:07,275
Both of them waived their rights, and they were brought back to England.

152
00:12:07,275 --> 00:12:14,932
They landed at Liverpool on the 27th of August, aboard the Megantic, to crowds that had been following the story for weeks.

153
00:12:16,782 --> 00:12:25,917
Crippen's trial opened at the Old Bailey on the 18th of October, 1910, before the Lord Chief Justice, Lord Alverstone.

154
00:12:25,917 --> 00:12:27,466
It lasted four days.

155
00:12:27,916 --> 00:12:30,021
The defence had a clear argument.

156
00:12:30,021 --> 00:12:33,019
Cora had left for America, as Crippen had said.

157
00:12:33,019 --> 00:12:40,101
And the remains, they suggested, could have been under the floor before the Crippens ever moved into the house.

158
00:12:40,101 --> 00:12:45,078
If that were true, then whatever lay in the cellar had nothing to do with him.

159
00:12:45,528 --> 00:12:48,242
The prosecution answered with science.

160
00:12:48,242 --> 00:13:01,239
A young pathologist named Bernard Spilsbury examined a piece of skin from the remains and identified what he said was a scar, consistent with an operation Cora was known to have had.

161
00:13:01,239 --> 00:13:05,024
The defence argued that it was only a fold of tissue.

162
00:13:05,024 --> 00:13:06,452
The jury heard both.

163
00:13:06,903 --> 00:13:09,155
Then there was the pyjama jacket.

164
00:13:09,155 --> 00:13:13,661
A matching pair of pyjama trousers was found in Crippen's bedroom.

165
00:13:13,661 --> 00:13:23,559
A witness from the makers, Jones Brothers, said the material had not been sold before 1908, long after the Crippens moved into Hilldrop Crescent.

166
00:13:23,559 --> 00:13:28,610
Whoever was buried in that cellar had been buried there in Crippen's time.

167
00:13:29,061 --> 00:13:30,840
And there was the hyoscine.

168
00:13:30,840 --> 00:13:36,641
Crippen had bought it, shortly before his wife was last seen, and it was in the remains.

169
00:13:36,641 --> 00:13:41,651
He never gave an account of what he had done with it that the jury accepted.

170
00:13:42,101 --> 00:13:46,504
The jury retired, and came back after twenty-seven minutes.

171
00:13:46,504 --> 00:13:47,026
Guilty.

172
00:13:47,026 --> 00:13:56,129
Throughout the trial, observers noted, Crippen showed little concern for himself, and a great deal for Ethel's reputation.

173
00:13:56,580 --> 00:14:01,336
Ethel Le Neve was tried separately, as an accessory after the fact.

174
00:14:01,336 --> 00:14:02,614
She was acquitted.

175
00:14:03,065 --> 00:14:11,867
Hawley Harvey Crippen was hanged at Pentonville Prison at nine in the morning on Wednesday, the 23rd of November, 1910.

176
00:14:11,867 --> 00:14:16,157
At his request, a photograph of Ethel was buried with him.

177
00:14:16,157 --> 00:14:18,893
He never said why he killed his wife.

178
00:14:18,893 --> 00:14:21,704
Theories have been offered ever since.

179
00:14:21,704 --> 00:14:23,258
They remain theories.

180
00:14:25,106 --> 00:14:29,804
The newspapers called it the most sensational capture of the century.

181
00:14:29,804 --> 00:14:33,753
What made it sensational was not the detective work alone.

182
00:14:33,753 --> 00:14:39,812
It was a message that crossed the sea faster than the ship carrying the man it described.

183
00:14:39,812 --> 00:14:44,985
For the first time, a fugitive on the open ocean was no longer out of reach.

184
00:14:45,437 --> 00:14:49,787
Walter Dew resigned from the police not long after the case.

185
00:14:49,787 --> 00:14:58,198
Years later he wrote a memoir called I Caught Crippen, and admitted in it that his memory of some details had faded.

186
00:14:58,198 --> 00:15:05,158
Henry Kendall went on to command the liner Empress of Ireland, and survived her sinking in 1914.

187
00:15:05,609 --> 00:15:14,552
Ethel Le Neve left for Canada, came back to London under another name, married, and raised two children in Croydon.

188
00:15:14,552 --> 00:15:15,875
She died in 1967.

189
00:15:16,325 --> 00:15:18,835
The case has not entirely rested.

190
00:15:18,835 --> 00:15:34,429
In 2007, a team led by the American forensic scientist David Foran reported that DNA from a century-old slide of the cellar remains did not match Cora's living relatives, and suggested the tissue was male.

191
00:15:34,429 --> 00:15:37,624
Others disputed the method and the sample.

192
00:15:37,624 --> 00:15:44,927
In 2009, the Criminal Cases Review Commission declined to refer the case to the Court of Appeal.

193
00:15:44,927 --> 00:15:46,600
The conviction stands.

194
00:15:48,051 --> 00:15:53,025
And behind all of it was a woman whose name is often left until last.

195
00:15:53,025 --> 00:16:00,955
Cora Crippen, Belle Elmore, a music hall singer whose friends refused to accept that she had simply gone away.

196
00:16:00,955 --> 00:16:05,569
If they had stayed quiet, no one would have asked the questions.

197
00:16:05,569 --> 00:16:08,165
She is remembered here with respect.

198
00:16:09,615 --> 00:16:11,632
This case file is closed.

199
00:16:11,632 --> 00:16:15,022
New case files arrive on Precinct Archive.
